Sign a new game build's page container on the device; take the developer identity once.

Local signing needed a page container built and signed on a Mac for each build of a game, so
every game update meant the Mac again. The app now makes it itself
(launcher/Pocket/PocketLocalSigning.m, with Tolkara's own signer, tools/sign_guest_local.m):
when a game's executable has no container of its own (Documents/LocalSigning/<sha256>.dylib),
the library launch and --native-startup build one from its __TEXT and sign it with the
developer identity in the app's keychain (ad hoc in the simulator), before looking for the
container as before. A failure says why in the alert or refusal it already showed. The
identity arrives once: tools/install.sh, with Local signing, runs tools/give_signing_identity.sh,
which copies it in a PKCS#12 file under a random passphrase and launches the app with the
passphrase; the app imports it at scene connection and deletes the file. Upstream candidate:
the on-device signing the container's documentation plans.

